# How the {{ twin_name }} twin checks itself

At every check the twin measures, on its own clock, whether it still keeps what was agreed, and leaves one state. Nothing here needs you to act; this file says what each number means and where to read it, so that you can explain the state to the user.

Notation: Δ = {{ fidelity.interval }} (the time between checks); W = {{ fidelity.window }} (the window each check looks back over); δ the sync interval{% if interval %} ({{ interval }}){% endif %}; c the time of a check, a whole multiple of Δ on the twin's clock. For a forecast question q: h its horizon, ε its tolerance, m its error metric, and the active model of its specification.

{% if fidelity.scored %}
| Forecast question | Horizon h | Tolerance ε | Model specification |
|---|---|---|---|
{% for q in fidelity.scored %}
| `{{ q.id }}` | {{ q.horizon or "-" }} | {{ q.tolerance or "-" }} | `{{ q.model }}` |
{% endfor %}

## The error of one forecast

Each forecast origin o = Δ·⌊(c − h)/Δ⌋ is scored once, at the first check with c ≥ o + h, and only if the active model was fitted on data that ends at or before o (it has not seen what it predicts). The twin forecasts from the version at o, as the model would have then, and compares with what was observed:

e(o) = m(forecast, observed) over every series and every step in (o, o + h].

A series whose signal was suspect at any time in that span is left out. Per origin the twin keeps e(o), the number of values n, and per series the mean signed residual d_s(o) (observed less forecast) and the last forecast value.

Read: `GET /fidelity/questions/{id}/errors` (one row per origin: `origin`, `scored_at`, `error`, `n`, `model`, `in_window`, `check`).

## Window error and breach

E(c) = the mean of e(o) over the scored origins with o + h in (c − W, c] and forecast by the model active then after the end of its data; n_q is their number.

- **Breach** of q: n_q ≥ 3 and E(c) > ε. It opens a `breach` incident, and the twin corrects (below).
- With fewer than 3 such origins nothing is concluded: the error is reported and no breach is raised.

Read: `GET /fidelity`, per question in `questions`: `error` (the newest origin), `window_error`, `window_n`, `tolerance`, `metric`, `breach`; `GET /fidelity/checks/{uid}` for what a past check measured.

## Warning

For each newly scored origin: x = e(o)/ε, and the running sum S ← max(0, S + x − 0.8). A warning is raised when S > 1.0: the twin re-fits the active model once on recent data without leaving `healthy`, sets S back to 0, and raises no other warning for that question within W. A warning catches a drift before it becomes a breach.

Read: `GET /fidelity`, `warning_sum` per question.

## Correction

A `breach` incident takes one step per check, each a job of its own (kind `correction`, in `GET /lifecycle/jobs?kind=correction`):

1. Re-fit: evaluate the active candidate again on recent data, at most {{ fidelity.recalibrate_attempts }} times. Accepted: it is made active and the twin waits for evidence.
{% if fidelity.reselect %}
2. Reselect: evaluate every candidate of the specification on recent data; the best accepted one is made active.
{% endif %}
{% if fidelity.agent_remodel %}
3. Ask the managing agent to revise the model: the incident is `remodel_requested` and the notification `twin.fidelity.remodel_requested` goes out (`reference/remodel.md`). A model of the specification registered or changed after the request sends the incident back to reselecting; meanwhile the twin goes on with whatever is left of its own rounds of steps 1 and 2, and only {{ fidelity.remodel_timeout }} without a model that holds makes it `failed`.
{% endif %}

"Recent data" is the frame from c − (the specification's initial window + W) to c, with forecast origins in [c − W, c − h], judged exactly as the specification's acceptance says; the backtest is recorded with scope `recent` (`GET /models/{uid}/backtests`).

Evidence: once a correction made a model active whose data ends at f (the incident's `awaiting`), the twin waits for n′ ≥ 3 scored origins with o ≥ f. Their mean E′ ≤ ε resolves the incident; E′ > ε means the correction did not hold, and the next step is taken. A round (`cycle`) that ends without a resolution is followed by the next, from the first step on newer data: round c ≥ 2 starts g·2^(c−2) after the previous one ended, with g = {{ fidelity.retry_backoff }}, at most {{ fidelity.retry_budget }} rounds. {% if fidelity.agent_remodel %}While a request to revise the model is open, the rounds go on and spending them does not fail the incident: only {{ fidelity.remodel_timeout }} from the request does. Otherwise, after{% else %}After{% endif %} the last round the incident is `failed`. A failed incident resolves when a model fitted after the failure has 3 scored origins with a mean ≤ ε, or by `POST /fidelity/incidents/{id}/retry`.

Read: `GET /fidelity/incidents/{id}`: `rung`, `attempt`, `cycle`, `awaiting`, `requested_at`, `detail.next_cycle_at` (when the next round is due, if one is), and `steps`, every step with its outcome, its `cycle` and `next_cycle_at`. A correction that was not accepted is a `result` step with `accepted: false` and `reason`: `code` (`not_accepted`, `too_little_history`, `timeout`, `fit_failed`, `nothing_to_evaluate`, `worker_refused`, `unexpected`, or the code of the error that stopped it), `message`, and `numbers` (the acceptance `metric` and `threshold`, and per candidate its `error`, `baseline_error`, `skill`, whether it was `accepted` and the rule's `reasons`).
{% endif %}

## Staleness

{% if fidelity.stale %}
A question is **stale** when its `fresh` is false at the check: the newest data of the source it waits for longest is older, on the twin's clock, than the question's limit. A `stale` incident opens and resolves by itself when fresh data arrives; after {{ fidelity.retry_budget }} stale checks in a row it is `failed`.

Read: `/meta/health`, `sync.questions` (`fresh`, `freshness_s`, `staleness`, `source`); `GET /sync/sources` for why a source is behind.
{% else %}
No question of this twin sets a limit on the age of its data, so no question is ever stale.
{% endif %}

## Suspect signals

A signal is one numeric observed property of one object. The twin tests two things.

**Stuck.** Over the look-back L = (c − max(W, 96 δ), c], reading the raw readings (also those sync kept on a suspect signal): t_last is the signal's last change; n is the number of whole sync intervals in (t_last, c] in which its source kept delivering (the source was `ok` with data newer than c − δ). Before t_last, of k intervals, u had no change. The signal is tested only when k − u ≥ 20. A **smooth** signal is not tested by the length of its run (a smooth level is flat at each turning point, which says nothing about its sensor): of its m pairs of consecutive changes, s keep their sign, and with m ≥ 10 a one-sided binomial test of s against m/2 gives p < 0.01. Once its run reaches n ≥ 3, the question that would infer it forecasts it, once for the run, from O = the end of the interval of its last change (never from an inferred value), and each check reads that forecast at the lead L = intervals since O: it is **stuck** when |f(O, L) − r| > m, r the unchanged reading and m half the question's tolerance (ε/2 for mae, rmse or max_abs; ε/2·|r| for mape), so a frozen reading is flagged before it is wrong by the tolerance; its inferred value is then that same forecast. With no such question, another metric, a refused forecast, L past the question's horizon or a value that is not finite, it is not tested (the check's `signals.forecasts` entry says why in `refused`; it also gives `question`, `origin`, `lead`, `value`, `reading`, `margin`). Any other signal: p₀ = (u + 1)/(k + 2), and over pairs of consecutive delivered intervals the transitions change→change, change→none, none→change, none→none are counted; when a G-test finds the intervals without change clustered (p < 0.01, and a run is likelier to go on than to start), b = P(none | change) and a = P(none | none) (each shrunk toward p₀ with weight 2), otherwise a = b = p₀. It is **stuck** when n ≥ 3 and P = b·aⁿ⁻¹ < 10⁻⁷: about one false alarm per series in 3,500 months of 15-minute checks. The check's record gives the numbers (`signs`, `transitions`, `model`, `b`, `a`, `p`, `needed`: the run that would be stuck).
{% if fidelity.scored %}

**Biased.** For each series of a forecast question's target with at least 8 scored origins: over its earlier origins (the newest 3 left out) the median m and the spread σ = 1.4826·MAD of d_s, with σ ≥ 0.1·ε; then z = (d_s(o) − m)/σ feeds a two-sided CUSUM with k = 0.5 and H = 5. A series alarms when |S| > H, on the side of S, and the newest 3 depart from m by at least ε on average (a shift smaller than the tolerance is not material). It is **biased** when it alarms in the question that would infer it (the forecast question on its property with the shortest horizon) and fewer than half of that question's series alarm at the same check (when most do, it is the model that is off, and the breach logic has it), and every other forecast question on the same property tested it at that check and alarms on the same side: a sensor that reads wrong disagrees with every model of its property; a model that is wrong for one series disagrees alone. When another question has not tested it (too few scored origins), does not alarm or alarms on the other side, the series is not flagged; the check's record lists it under `signals.uncorroborated` with that question (`by`) and why (`not_tested`, `no_alarm`, `other_side`).
{% endif %}

What a suspect signal does:

- Sync stops writing that property of that object. The value in force stays, the raw reading is kept on the signal (`reading`, `reading_at`), not on the object.
{% if fidelity.scored %}
- It is inferred by one validated forecast question on that target: first one whose model declares it does not read the signal, by shortest horizon; else, as every shipped family reads its own series, the one with the longest horizon H. A model reads its features and what its specification lists in `reads` (every observed property a prediction of it reads from the twin besides its features); a shipped family also reads its own series, and code of the twin's own that lists no `reads` is taken to read every property, so it forecasts a signal once and none of its origins is scored while any signal is suspect. The signal's `detail.inference.chosen` says which and why (`does_not_read_it`, `reads_it_longest_horizon`). The forecast is made from the newest origin at which every input of that model was observed: none suspect, inferred, unknown, or frozen in the run of a stuck signal, which began at its last change (`run_since` on its episode). A model that does not read the signal forecasts it again at every check from the newest such origin, so it never rests on inferred values and stays fresh; one that reads it forecasts it once. At each check the twin writes the forecast's value for now in its place, at the lead it has reached; its provenance says kind `inferred` and the model as source (`GET /objects/{type}/{uid}?include=provenance`). Without such a question, or such an origin in the window, its value is unknown at once.
- Once the lead passes H, or when the value is not finite or lies outside the property's range (the ontology's, or the range observed over the window widened on each side by max(half its width, ε)), nothing is inferred: the value is **unknown** until the signal clears. The object holds no value for that property, its provenance `inferred` from the signal with confidence 0; the signal's `value` is `unknown`, with `unknown.why` (`horizon_passed`, `not_finite`, `out_of_range`, `no_inference`) and the questions left without an answer, also on its incident. `twin.fidelity.signal_unknown` is notified once.
{% else %}
- No forecast question covers it: its value is **unknown** until it clears (`unknown.why`: `no_inference`), and `twin.fidelity.signal_unknown` is notified once.
{% endif %}
- Whatever reads an unknown value is refused with `fidelity.input_unknown`, naming the signal: a forecast of a model whose target, feature or `reads` it is (any value, for code that lists no `reads`), a function reading it, a question about it. No autonomous action is executed whose control's target is unknown on its object; its `reasons` name the signal.{% if fidelity.scored %} A forecast origin at which a property the question's model reads, for any object, was unknown, inferred or a frozen reading of a stuck run is not scored (`reason`: `input_unknown`, `input_inferred` or `input_frozen`; `withheld` names the signals); a series whose own value was not observed is left out of the score, as every suspect series is.{% endif %}
- The signal is left out of every forecast error from the time it was flagged, a stuck one from the start of its run. Origins scored before a stuck flag that rested on its frozen readings leave the window error once the flag comes (`window.set_aside`): no breach and no correction rests on them.
- A stuck signal clears itself when its raw readings move again: at least 3 intervals with a change since the flag, the run without a change in progress shorter than 3 intervals, and the stuck test no longer calling it stuck. A flag again needs a whole new run of the length that is stuck, so the two cannot alternate from one check to the next.{% if fidelity.scored %} A biased one clears after 3 checks in a row in which the raw reading lies within 2·ε of the inferred value; a check with nothing inferred to compare with neither counts nor resets, so a biased signal whose value is unknown (its model reads it, and the lead passed H) stays suspect until an operator clears it once the sensor is mended.{% endif %} The `cleared` step on its incident carries the numbers it cleared on. Or by `POST /fidelity/signals/{uid}/clear`, which resolves its incident.

Read: `GET /fidelity/signals?status=suspect` (`reason`: `stuck` or `biased`; `since`, `value` (`inferred` or `unknown`), `unknown`, `value_at_flag`, `reading`, `clear_checks`, `detail`: `inference`, `estimate`, `episodes`).

## The state

- `failed` if any incident is `failed`;
- otherwise `correcting` if any incident is open or `remodel_requested`;
- otherwise `healthy` once a check has completed;
- `unknown` before the first check.

Freshness changes the state through stale incidents; the coverage (how many questions are validated, provisional, failing), the state of each source and the rows set aside are reported by the check and do not change the state.

Read the whole picture, with the helpers of `reference/recipes.md` (Setup):

```python
fidelity = get("/fidelity").json()
print(fidelity["state"], fidelity["since"], fidelity["check"])
for q in fidelity["questions"]:
    print(q["id"], q["error"], q["window_error"], q["window_n"], q["tolerance"], q["breach"], q["reason"])
for incident in fidelity["incidents"]:            # every incident not resolved
    print(incident["uid"], incident["kind"], incident["subject"], incident["status"], incident["rung"])
for signal in fidelity["suspect_signals"]:
    print(signal["uid"], signal["property"], signal["object"], signal["reason"], signal["since"])
```
